 $100 \\times A + 10 \\times B + C$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e7465922015f399a7547e7c1d07849b854c39af7af6272375cf9401c54a455e0"},"seconds":54.950862407684326,"seed":116269257140563199,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of the first $25$ positive integers and then match it with the given form $26A7114B4C0$ to find the values of $A$, $B$, and $C$.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the LCM of the first 25 positive integers\n\nThe LCM of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the integers in the set. To find the LCM of the first 25 positive integers, we need the highest powers of all prime numbers that are less than or equal to 25.\n\nThe primes less than or equal to 25 are: \\(2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23\\).\n\n- \\(2^4 = 16\\) (since \\(2^4 \\leq 25 < 2^5\\))\n- \\(3^3 = 27\\) is too large, so we use \\(3^2 = 9\\)\n- \\(5^2 = 25\\) (since \\(5^2 \\leq 25 < 5^3\\))\n- \\(7^1 = 7\\)\n- \\(11^1 = 11\\)\n- \\(13^1 = 13\\)\n- \\(17^1 = 17\\)\n- \\(19^1 = 19\\)\n- \\(23^1 = 23\\)\n\nTherefore, the LCM of the first 25 positive integers is:\n\\[\n2^4 \\times 3^2 \\times 5^2 \\times 7 \\times 11 \\times 13 \\times 17 \\times 19 \\times 23\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the LCM\n\nLet's calculate the product:\n\\[\n2^4 = 16\n\\]\n\\[\n3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n7 = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n11 = 11\n\\]\n\\[\n13 = 13\n\\]\n\\[\n17 = 17\n\\]\n\\[\n19 = 19\n\\]\n\\[\n23 = 23\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply these together:\n\\[\n16 \\times 9 = 144\n\\]\n\\[\n144 \\times 25 = 3600\n\\]\n\\[\n3600 \\times 7 = 25200\n\\]\n\\[\n25200 \\times 11 = 277200\n\\]\n\\[\n277200 \\times 13 = 3603600\n\\]\n\\[\n3603600 \\times 17 = 61261200\n\\]\n\\[\n61261200 \\times 19 = 1163962800\n\\]\n\\[\n1163962800 \\times 23 = 26771144400\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Match the LCM to the given form\n\nThe LCM we calculated is \\(26771144400\\). This matches the form \\(26A7114B4C0\\) where:\n- \\(A = 7\\)\n- \\(B = 4\\)\n- \\(C = 4\\)\n\n### Step 4: Calculate \\(100 \\times A + 10 \\times B + C\\)\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 7 + 10 \\times 4 + 4 = 700 + 40 + 4 = 744\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{744}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{744}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
